Scenario 1: Same-day replay burst
MQS sometimes sends the same quote message several times within a few milliseconds on the same day. Each copy lands with its own enqueue timestamp, microseconds apart. Ingestion dedup catches these copies because they are identical and arrive close together, so only one reaches Bronze, Silver and Gold. Every copy stays in Landing, but the DVLA retention pipeline is not impacted because each copy has a different timestamp. There is no downstream impact; this scenario is contained.

Scenario 2: Cross-day replay
MQS resends a quote message one or more days after the original, with a new enqueue timestamp. The resend arrives outside the dedup window, so it is treated as a new event and flows through Bronze, Silverish, Silver and Gold. In our sample, one quote first sent on 22 Sep and resent on 23 and 25 Sep now appears three times in Silver and in Gold, once for each day it arrived. Pricing ART, which reads the DLT views built on Bronze, is expected to show the duplicates too. The DVLA retention pipeline is not impacted because the copies have different timestamps. The impact is over-counted quotes in reporting, and it recurs.

Scenario 3: Same quote and message type with different quote-step values
MQS sends more than one version of the same quote message, with different quote-step values. This is a source defect: MQS had reported it fixed, but it recurred between 8 and 30 Sep 2026. Because the records are not identical, dedup treats each version as a separate event, and all versions flow through to Silver and Gold. The result is conflicting versions of one quote, with no rule yet for which one is correct. Pricing ART is expected to show the variants. The DVLA retention pipeline is not impacted because the records differ. The impact is a data-correctness problem in Silver and Gold that needs MQS to fix at source.

Scenario 4: Historical header-fix reload
During the header-issue fix, the same historical data was loaded into Landing more than once. These loads bypassed the normal streaming path, so dedup did not remove the copies. The duplicates are now in Bronze, Enrichment, Silver and Gold for the affected period. The DVLA retention pipeline failed on these records (confirmed). The issue is one-off rather than ongoing, so it needs a single cleanup and a successful rerun of the DVLA retention pipeline.

Scenario 5: Exact upstream replay
For certain quotes, the upstream system resends a message that is an exact copy of the original, including the same enqueue timestamp. Dedup removes the copy before Bronze, so Bronze, Silver and Gold are clean. However, both copies stay in Landing, and the DVLA retention pipeline scans Landing. When it finds two copies of a quote with exactly the same timestamp, the whole daily run is rejected (confirmed). That means DVLA data is not removed for any quote in that batch, which is a compliance risk. Because the behaviour is ongoing, each new exact resend causes the failure again until MQS stops the resends or the pipeline can handle them.
